      {
        "id": 145089,
        "name": "BlueHairedMeerkat",
        "username": "BlueHairedMeerkat",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/bluehairedmeerkat/{size}/1967_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-09-02T21:53:09.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"arenson9\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/48/1900_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> arenson9:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do you feel about it?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Weeelllll…</p>\n<p>On the one hand, it was a fantastically enjoyable three weeks. We did all sorts of weird and cool things, plus spending time with so many wonderful people was great. On the other hand, it was tiring having to be 'on' for sixteen hours a day, and having to deal with producers (not all of them, but enough) trying to manufacture drama and such. On the third hand, I have made a bunch of really good friends out of it, and watching in on television was incredibly surreal and entertaining.</p>\n<p>As for the show itself? I enjoyed watching it, certainly. I think it was decent fluff television. Its view of geek culture is a bit reductionist, certainly, but I think ultimately it's a net positive. Not that it didn't squander a lot of opportunities, but geekdom was genuinely celebrated rather than just mocked.</p>\n<p>Finally, here's my favourite reaction to the finale:&nbsp;<a href=\"https://twitter.com/hannahbanana484/status/638062968022921216\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">x.com</a></p>",
